OAI_PAPER({"slug": "An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026", "title": "An explicit counterexample to the Auslander-Reiten conjecture", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "199", "family_title": "Counterexamples to Auslander–Reiten, Tachikawa and related homological conjectures", "abstract_html": "We disprove the Auslander–Reiten conjecture for Artin algebras. We construct a finite-dimensional algebra Λ over \\(k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)\\) and a finite-dimensional nonprojective left module <i>Z</i> such that \\(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Ext}}\\nolimits ^i_\\Lambda(Z,Z)=\\mathop{\\mathrm{Ext}}\\nolimits ^i_\\Lambda(Z,\\Lambda)=0\\) for every <i>i</i> &amp;gt; 0. The module is Gorenstein-projective, so the same example also disproves the Gorenstein-projective conjecture. Both counterexamples persist after every extension of <i>k</i>.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k=\\mathbb F _2(q,H_1,H_2)\\), where \\(q,H_1,H_2\\) are algebraically independent.</p><p class=\"li\">All algebras and modules constructed below are finite-dimensional over their indicated field. Modules are left modules unless a side is specified.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(DV=\\operatorname{Hom}_k(V,k)\\) and \\(R^e=R\\otimes_kR^{\\mathrm{op}}\\). Tensor products without a subscript are over \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A complex of finite projective \\(R\\)-modules is totally acyclic if it is exact and remains exact after applying \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_R(-,R)\\). A module is Gorenstein-projective if it is a cokernel in such a doubly infinite complex.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist a finite-dimensional \\(k\\)-algebra \\(\\Lambda\\) and a finite-dimensional nonprojective left \\(\\Lambda\\)-module \\(Z\\) such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i_\\Lambda(Z,Z)\n=\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i_\\Lambda(Z,\\Lambda)\n=\n0\n\\qquad(i\\geq 1).\n\\]\nThe module \\(Z\\) is Gorenstein-projective. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\Lambda/\\operatorname{rad}\\Lambda\\simeq k^8,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rad}(\\Lambda)^4\\neq 0.\n\\]\nThese conclusions persist after every field extension of \\(k\\).</p><p>Consequently, the Auslander–Reiten conjecture and its restriction to Gorenstein-projective modules fail for Artin algebras, including finite-dimensional algebras over an algebraically closed field of characteristic two. The generator formulation also fails: \\(Z\\oplus\\Lambda\\) is a nonprojective generator with no positive self-extensions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 2 constructs the ten-dimensional algebra \\(C\\), its symmetric trivial extension \\(T\\), and the degree-three cocycle \\(p\\), including its twisted boundary identity.</p><p>Proposition 3 constructs the symmetric algebra\n\\[\nA=T\\otimes_kT\n\\]\nand the simple module \\(X=s\\otimes_ks\\), with nonnegative stable self-extension algebra \\(k[\\tau_1,\\tau_2]\\).</p><p>Proposition 4 constructs the finite side-projective two-cone bimodule \\(\\mathcal C\\) and computes\n\\[\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,\\mathcal CX[a])\n=\n\\begin{cases}\nk,&amp;a=0,3,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nProposition 5 constructs the lifted maps from the two twists \\(U_{H_1},U_{H_2}\\) to \\(\\mathcal C[3]\\), with nonzero evaluated top projections.</p><p>Proposition 6 combines their normalized evaluations into the finite side-projective bimodule \\(F\\) and stable map \\(v:X\\to FX\\). It proves that the comparison map \\(\\delta^0\\) is surjective with nonzero kernel, and that every \\(\\delta^a\\), \\(a&gt;0\\), is an isomorphism.</p><p>Apply Proposition 1. The algebra\n\\[\n\\Lambda=\\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;0\\\\F&amp;A\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nis finite-dimensional, with\n\\[\n\\dim_k\\Lambda=800+\\dim_kF.\n\\]\nThe finite quotient (3.2) gives a nonprojective Gorenstein-projective module \\(Z\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i_\\Lambda(Z,Z)\n=\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i_\\Lambda(Z,\\Lambda)\n=\n0\n\\qquad(i&gt;0).\n\\]\nThe degree-one vanishing uses the surjectivity of \\(\\delta^0\\), separately from positive-degree invertibility.</p><p>Proposition 7 gives\n\\[\n\\Lambda/\\operatorname{rad}\\Lambda\\simeq k^8,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rad}(\\Lambda)^4\\neq0.\n\\]\nProposition 8 proves persistence under every field extension. In particular, one may extend to an algebraic closure of \\(k\\), which still contains the transcendental parameters.</p><p>A finite-dimensional algebra over a field is an Artin algebra. Thus \\(Z\\) violates the Auslander–Reiten conjecture. Since it is Gorenstein-projective and has no positive self-extensions, it also violates the Gorenstein-projective conjecture.</p><p>Finally, Ext is additive in both arguments, and all positive Ext groups whose first argument is \\(\\Lambda\\) vanish. Hence \\(Z\\oplus\\Lambda\\) is a nonprojective generator with no positive self-extensions, giving the stated failure of the generator formulation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 3.1, “Triangular conversion.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In this proposition, \\(k\\) may be any field of characteristic two.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a finite-dimensional symmetric \\(k\\)-algebra. Thus there is a linear form \\(t:A\\to k\\) such that \\(t(ab)=t(ba)\\) and \\((a,b)\\mapsto t(ab)\\) is nondegenerate.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be a finite \\(A\\)-bimodule projective separately as a left and as a right \\(A\\)-module, and let \\(X\\) be a finite left \\(A\\)-module. Write \\(FM=F\\otimes_AM\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite algebra \\(R\\), write\n\\[\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_R(M,N)\n=\n\\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,N)/\n\\{\\text{maps factoring through finite projectives}\\}.\n\\]\nOver a symmetric algebra, \\(M[-1]=\\Omega M\\) and \\(M[1]\\) is a cosyzygy. These are inverse shifts on the stable category.</p><p class=\"li\">Complexes have homological differentials \\(d_i:P_i\\to P_{i-1}\\) and shifts \\(P[a]_i=P_{i-a}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\nv\\in\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,FX),\n\\qquad\nH^a=\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,X[a]),\n\\qquad\nK^a=\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,(FX)[a]).\n\\]\nTensoring with \\(F\\) is exact and preserves projectives, so it acts on stable maps and commutes with shifts.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n\\delta^a:H^a\\oplus H^a\\longrightarrow K^a,\n\\qquad\n(g,j)\\longmapsto F(g)v+v[a]j.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nAssume that \\(\\delta^0\\) is surjective with nonzero kernel and that \\(\\delta^a\\) is an isomorphism for every \\(a&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose an actual representative \\(v_0:X\\to FX\\) of \\(v\\) and an embedding \\(i:X\\hookrightarrow Q\\) into a finite projective \\(A\\)-module.</p><p class=\"li\">A left module over\n\\[\n\\Lambda=\\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;0\\\\F&amp;A\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nis written as a triple \\((M,N,\\eta)\\), where \\(\\eta:F\\otimes_AM\\to N\\), with action\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;0\\\\f&amp;b\\end{pmatrix}(m,n)\n=\n(am,\\eta(f\\otimes m)+bn).\n\\]\nA morphism \\((\\alpha,\\beta)\\) satisfies \\(\\beta\\eta=\\eta'(F\\otimes\\alpha)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Define\n\\[\nY=(FX\\oplus Q)/\\{(v_0(x),i(x)):x\\in X\\},\n\\qquad\nZ=(X,Y,\\iota),\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\iota:FX\\to Y\\) is induced by the first summand. Then \\(Z\\) is a finite nonprojective Gorenstein-projective left \\(\\Lambda\\)-module, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^a_\\Lambda(Z,Z)\n=\n\\operatorname{Ext}^a_\\Lambda(Z,\\Lambda)\n=\n0\n\\qquad(a&gt;0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The symmetrizing form identifies \\(A\\simeq DA\\) as bimodules, so finite projective \\(A\\)-modules are injective. Every finite module embeds into a finite projective by dualizing a projective surjection onto its dual. Syzygies and cosyzygies therefore give inverse stable shifts.</p><p>A short exact sequence gives a stable triangle and long exact stable Hom sequences in all integer degrees. At degree zero, maps through a projective lift through a surjection and extend across an injection, so the ordinary sequences remain exact after quotienting by projective factorizations. Shifting gives all other degrees. In particular,\n\\[\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(M,N[a])\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{Ext}^a_A(M,N)\n\\qquad(a&gt;0).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nA concrete cone of \\(u:M\\to N\\) is the cokernel of \\((u,i):M\\to N\\oplus I\\), where \\(i\\) embeds \\(M\\) into a projective.</p><p>Since \\(i\\) in (3.2) is injective, \\(\\iota\\) is injective and\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow FX\\xrightarrow{\\iota}Y\\longrightarrow Q/i(X)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nAll modules are finite, and\n\\[\n\\dim_k\\Lambda=2\\dim_kA+\\dim_kF.\n\\]</p><p>Define the column functors\n\\[\nE(M)=(M,FM,\\operatorname{id}),\n\\qquad\nJ(N)=(0,N,0).\n\\]\nThe functor \\(E\\) is exact because \\(F\\) is right projective; \\(J\\) is exact directly. Both preserve finite projectives, and\n\\[\n\\Lambda=E(A)\\oplus J(A).\n\\]\nThe morphism condition gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Lambda(E(M),E(M'))&amp;\\simeq\\operatorname{Hom}_A(M,M'),\\\\\n\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Lambda(J(N),J(N'))&amp;\\simeq\\operatorname{Hom}_A(N,N'),\\\\\n\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Lambda(E(M),J(N))&amp;=0,\\\\\n\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Lambda(J(N),E(M))&amp;\\simeq\\operatorname{Hom}_A(N,FM).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(3.6)\n\\]\nIn the first line the lower component is \\(F(\\alpha)\\). The third line holds termwise for complexes in every shift.</p><p>Choose a complete resolution \\(P_\\bullet\\) of \\(X\\) with \\(P_{-1}=Q\\) and \\(d_0=i\\epsilon_P\\), by splicing a projective resolution with a projective-injective coresolution. Put\n\\[\nG_0=E(P_\\bullet),\n\\qquad\nG_1=J(P_\\bullet).\n\\]\nThese are exact complexes of finite projective \\(\\Lambda\\)-modules. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Lambda(G_0,\\Lambda)\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{Hom}_A(P_\\bullet,A),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Lambda(G_1,\\Lambda)\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{Hom}_A(P_\\bullet,F)\\oplus\n\\operatorname{Hom}_A(P_\\bullet,A).\n\\]\nThese complexes are exact because \\(F\\) and \\(A\\) are projective-injective as left \\(A\\)-modules. Thus \\(G_0,G_1\\) are totally acyclic. Also \\(F\\otimes_AP_\\bullet\\) is a complete resolution of \\(FX\\): right projectivity gives exactness, left projectivity gives projective terms, and self-injectivity of \\(A\\) gives Hom exactness.</p><p>Lemma 1.1 and (3.6) identify\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH^a\\operatorname{End}^\\bullet_\\Lambda(G_0)&amp;\\simeq H^a,\\\\\nH^a\\operatorname{End}^\\bullet_\\Lambda(G_1)&amp;\\simeq H^a,\\\\\nH^a\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_\\Lambda(G_1,G_0)&amp;\\simeq K^a,\\\\\n\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_\\Lambda(G_0,G_1)&amp;=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(3.7)\n\\]\nLift \\(v_0\\) to a chain map \\(V:G_1\\to G_0\\) using Lemma 1.1, and form\n\\[\nP_Z=\\operatorname{Cone}(V),\n\\qquad\n(P_Z)_j=(G_0)_j\\oplus(G_1)_{j-1},\n\\qquad\nd^{P_Z}_j=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nd^{G_0}&amp;V_{j-1}\\\\\n0&amp;d^{G_1}\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe degreewise split cone sequence shows that \\(P_Z\\) is totally acyclic with finite projective terms.</p><p>Its degree-zero cokernel is exactly \\(Z\\). The upper component is \\(X\\). For the lower component, quotient \\(F\\otimes_AP_0\\) by \\(\\operatorname{im}(F\\otimes_Ad_1)\\), obtaining \\(FX\\). The remaining relations are \\((v_0(x),i(x))\\), because \\(V_0\\) induces \\(v_0\\) and \\(d_0=i\\epsilon_P\\). Thus the lower component is \\(Y\\), with structure map \\(\\iota\\).</p><p>Every graded endomorphism of \\(P_Z\\) has two diagonal blocks and one block from \\(G_1[1]\\) to \\(G_0\\); the other direction vanishes by (3.6). Projection to the diagonal gives\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_\\Lambda(G_1[1],G_0)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{End}^\\bullet_\\Lambda(P_Z)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{End}^\\bullet_\\Lambda(G_0)\\oplus\n\\operatorname{End}^\\bullet_\\Lambda(G_1[1])\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nIt is split in each graded degree, and\n\\[\nH^a\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_\\Lambda(G_1[1],G_0)=K^{a-1}.\n\\]\nThe connecting map from diagonal degree \\(a\\) to off-diagonal degree \\(a+1\\) is \\(\\delta^a\\). Indeed, the cross term of the differential of a diagonal lift is composition with \\(V\\) on its two sides. The \\(E\\)-diagonal acts by \\(F(g)\\), and the \\(J\\)-diagonal by precomposition with \\(j\\). Composition compatibility in Lemma 1.1 identifies this with \\(F(g)v+v[a]j\\).</p><p>The long exact sequence yields\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{coker}\\delta^{a-1}\n\\longrightarrow H^a\\operatorname{End}^\\bullet_\\Lambda(P_Z)\n\\longrightarrow\\ker\\delta^a\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nFor \\(a=1\\), surjectivity of \\(\\delta^0\\) and injectivity of \\(\\delta^1\\) make both outer terms zero. For \\(a&gt;1\\), both relevant comparison maps are isomorphisms. Lemma 1.1 therefore gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^a_\\Lambda(Z,Z)=0\n\\qquad(a&gt;0).\n\\]</p><p>At degree zero, (3.9) gives a surjection onto the nonzero \\(\\ker\\delta^0\\). Thus the stable endomorphism group of \\(Z\\) is nonzero. Its stable identity is nonzero, since a zero identity would annihilate every stable endomorphism. Hence \\(Z\\) is not projective.</p><p>Finally, total acyclicity makes \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Lambda(P_Z,\\Lambda)\\) exact. Its positive degrees compute \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^a_\\Lambda(Z,\\Lambda)\\), so these vanish. The cokernel \\(Z\\) is also the cycle \\(\\operatorname{im}d^{P_Z}_0=\\ker d^{P_Z}_{-1}\\), and is Gorenstein-projective.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, “Complete-resolution comparison.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, replace the symmetric algebra in Proposition 1 by an arbitrary finite-dimensional algebra \\(R\\) over a field of characteristic two.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_\\bullet\\) be a totally acyclic complex of finite projective left \\(R\\)-modules, and let \\(Q_\\bullet\\) be an exact complex of finite projective left \\(R\\)-modules.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nC_j(P_\\bullet)=\\operatorname{coker}(d^P_{j+1}:P_{j+1}\\to P_j),\n\\qquad\nM=C_0(P_\\bullet),\n\\qquad\nN=C_0(Q_\\bullet).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the full Hom complex\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}^a_R(P_\\bullet,Q_\\bullet)\n=\n\\prod_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}\\operatorname{Hom}_R(P_j,Q_{j-a}),\n\\qquad\n(\\partial f)_j=d^Q_{j-a}f_j+f_{j-1}d^P_j.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are natural, composition-compatible identifications\n\\[\nH^0\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_R(P_\\bullet,Q_\\bullet)\n\\simeq\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_R(M,N).\n\\]\nFor every integer \\(a\\),\n\\[\nH^a\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_R(P_\\bullet,Q_\\bullet)\n\\simeq\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_R(C_a(P_\\bullet),N)\n\\simeq\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_R(M,C_{-a}(Q_\\bullet)).\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nFor \\(a&gt;0\\), these groups are naturally \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^a_R(M,N)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Exactness identifies \\(C_j(P_\\bullet)\\) with \\(\\operatorname{im}d^P_j\\subset P_{j-1}\\). Total acyclicity supplies the following extension property: for every finite projective \\(T\\), a map\n\\[\n\\phi:\\operatorname{im}d^P_j\\longrightarrow T\n\\]\nextends to \\(P_{j-1}\\). Indeed, \\(\\phi d^P_j:P_j\\to T\\) is a cocycle in \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_R(P_\\bullet,T)\\). Exactness gives \\(\\psi:P_{j-1}\\to T\\) with \\(\\psi d^P_j=\\phi d^P_j\\). This does not assume that projective \\(R\\)-modules are injective.</p><p>A chain map induces a map of cokernels. Conversely, given \\(u:M\\to N\\), first lift \\(u\\epsilon_P\\) to \\(f_0:P_0\\to Q_0\\). Projectivity and exactness successively give \\(f_1,f_2,\\ldots\\) satisfying\n\\[\nd^Q_jf_j=f_{j-1}d^P_j.\n\\]\nTo extend in the other direction, \\(d^Q_0f_0\\) kills \\(\\operatorname{im}d^P_1=\\ker d^P_0\\). It induces a map \\(\\operatorname{im}d^P_0\\to Q_{-1}\\), which extends to \\(f_{-1}:P_{-1}\\to Q_{-1}\\) by the extension property. The same argument constructs \\(f_{-2},f_{-3},\\ldots\\).</p><p>A nullhomotopy has components \\(h_j:P_j\\to Q_{j+1}\\) and satisfies\n\\[\nf_j=d^Q_{j+1}h_j+h_{j-1}d^P_j.\n\\]\nOn cokernels, the first summand vanishes and the second factors through \\(P_{-1}\\). Thus a nullhomotopic map induces a projective factorization.</p><p>Conversely, suppose that the induced map factors as \\(M\\to T\\to N\\), with \\(T\\) finite projective. Extend \\(M\\to T\\) to \\(P_{-1}\\) by the extension property and lift \\(T\\to N\\) to \\(Q_0\\). Their composite is a homotopy component \\(h_{-1}\\) whose boundary induces the given factorization. Subtract this boundary. It remains to nullhomotope a chain map \\(f'\\) inducing zero on cokernels.</p><p>For this remaining map take \\(h_{-1}=0\\). Since \\(f'_0\\) has image in \\(\\operatorname{im}d^Q_1\\), projectivity gives \\(h_0:P_0\\to Q_1\\) with \\(d^Q_1h_0=f'_0\\). Proceeding upwards, the residual\n\\[\nf'_j+h_{j-1}d^P_j\n\\]\nlands in \\(\\ker d^Q_j=\\operatorname{im}d^Q_{j+1}\\), so it lifts to \\(h_j\\). Proceeding downwards from \\(j=-1\\), the residual\n\\[\nf'_j+d^Q_{j+1}h_j\n\\]\nkills \\(\\operatorname{im}d^P_{j+1}\\) by the equation already obtained at \\(j+1\\). It descends to \\(\\operatorname{im}d^P_j\\) and extends to \\(h_{j-1}:P_{j-1}\\to Q_j\\). These recursions define a homotopy in the full product (3.3).</p><p>This proves the degree-zero identification. Passing to cokernels respects composition, and uniqueness modulo homotopy gives composition compatibility.</p><p>Apply the degree-zero result to \\(P_\\bullet[-a]\\) or \\(Q_\\bullet[a]\\) to obtain (3.4). For \\(a&gt;0\\), \\(C_a(P_\\bullet)\\) is the \\(a\\)-th syzygy of \\(M\\). The projective-resolution definition of \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^a_R(M,N)\\) quotients \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_R(C_a(P_\\bullet),N)\\) by maps extending to \\(P_{a-1}\\). Such maps factor through a projective; conversely, every projective factorization extends to \\(P_{a-1}\\) by the extension property. The quotients coincide, including for \\(a=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "This proposition collects the construction and finite identities of Section 4.", "statement": "<p>This proposition collects the construction and finite identities of Section 4. Its constituent results have original labels Lemmas 4.1, 4.3, and 4.4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Return to \\(k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) have the following basis, arranged by corners:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|l@{\\qquad}c|l}\n\\text{corner}&amp;\\text{basis}&amp;\\text{corner}&amp;\\text{basis}\\\\ \\hline\neCe&amp;e,x,y,z&amp;eCf&amp;u,v\\\\\nfCe&amp;t,j&amp;fCf&amp;f,n .\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nThe elements \\(e,f\\) are orthogonal idempotents with sum \\(1\\). Apart from their corner actions, the complete list of nonzero products is\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nxy=qz,\\qquad yx=z,\\qquad xu=yu=v,\\qquad tx=j,\\qquad ty=q^2j,\\\\\nut=y+qx,\\qquad vt=qz,\\qquad uj=z,\\qquad\ntu=(1+q)n,\\qquad nt=qj,\\qquad un=v.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\qquad(4.2)\n\\]\nAll unspecified products are zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(L=DC\\), with\n\\[\n(a\\phi)(c)=\\phi(ca),\n\\qquad\n(\\phi a)(c)=\\phi(ac).\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nT=C\\ltimes L,\n\\qquad\n(a,\\phi)(b,\\psi)=(ab,a\\psi+\\phi b).\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nFor a lower-case basis letter \\(a\\), its upper-case letter \\(a^*\\) is the dual vector in \\(DC\\); for example, \\(j^*=J\\), \\(f^*=F\\), and \\((a^*)^*=a\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s\\) be the one-dimensional left \\(T\\)-module on which \\(f\\) acts as \\(1\\) and every other basis letter acts as zero. Let \\(s^r\\) be the right simple with the same character.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(I=ke\\oplus kf\\). Once \\(\\mathfrak r=\\operatorname{rad}T\\) is identified, use the normalized relative bar resolution\n\\[\nP_n=T\\otimes_I\\mathfrak r^{\\otimes_I n}\\otimes_IT\n\\quad(n\\geq0),\n\\qquad\nP_n=0\\quad(n&lt;0).\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nThe differential sums the products of adjacent factors. Write its tensors as \\(a_0[a_1|\\cdots|a_n]a_{n+1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Give lower-case letters weight \\(0\\) and upper-case letters weight \\(1\\). Let\n\\[\np:\\mathfrak r\\otimes_I\\mathfrak r\\otimes_I\\mathfrak r\\longrightarrow T\n\\]\nbe the cochain specified in Lemma 2.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rules define an associative algebra \\(C\\) of dimension \\(10\\). Its trivial extension \\(T\\) is symmetric of dimension \\(20\\). The cochain \\(p\\) is a weight-\\(-1\\) Hochschild cocycle whose evaluation on \\(s\\) is nonzero in \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^3_T(s,s)\\). It satisfies the twisted boundary identity (4.6) in Lemma 2.3 and gives the bar comparison map\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np:P&amp;\\longrightarrow P[3],\\\\\na_0[a_1|\\cdots|a_n]a_{n+1}\n&amp;\\longmapsto\na_0[a_1|\\cdots|a_{n-3}]\np(a_{n-2},a_{n-1},a_n)a_{n+1}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(4.8)\n\\]\nfor \\(n\\geq3\\), with value zero for \\(n&lt;3\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2 establish the algebra and symmetry assertions, and Lemma 2.3 establishes the cochain identities and nonzero evaluation.</p><p>For completeness, the relative bar terms in (4.5) are finite sums of projective bimodules \\(Ti\\otimes jT\\), with \\(i,j\\in\\{e,f\\}\\). Exactness follows from the contraction which replaces the first coefficient by its projection to \\(\\mathfrak r\\), inserts that projection as a new first bar, and puts \\(1\\) in the first coefficient. In augmented degree it sends \\(a\\) to \\(1[]a\\). Successive multiplications cancel to give \\(d\\sigma+\\sigma d=\\operatorname{id}\\); the \\(I\\)-component is balanced over \\(I\\). The contraction is right \\(T\\)-linear, so \\(P\\otimes_Ts\\) is a projective resolution of \\(s\\).</p><p>The suffix formula (4.8) is a chain map. Strict-prefix multiplications cancel, and the equation remaining on the last four bars is exactly the Hochschild cocycle equation. At the bottom index the target differential is zero. Composing its evaluation on \\(s\\) with the augmentation represents the nonzero class of Lemma 2.3.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(C\\), its basis, corner actions, and multiplication rules from Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These rules define an associative unital algebra of dimension \\(10\\). Its radical is the span of the eight non-idempotent basis vectors, and\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{rad}C)^5=0,\n\\qquad\nutut=q(1+q)z\\neq0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Give \\(u,t\\) degree \\(1\\), \\(x,y,n\\) degree \\(2\\), \\(v,j\\) degree \\(3\\), and \\(z\\) degree \\(4\\); give \\(e,f\\) degree \\(0\\). Every listed product is homogeneous.</p><p>Associativity involving an idempotent follows from the corners. For three non-idempotent basis vectors, incompatible corners or total degree greater than \\(4\\) force both bracketings to vanish. The remaining list is\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccccccccccc}\nabc&amp;utu&amp;tut&amp;xut&amp;yut&amp;ntu&amp;unt&amp;txu&amp;tyu&amp;utx&amp;uty&amp;tun\\\\ \\hline\n(ab)c=a(bc)\n&amp;(1+q)v&amp;q(1+q)j&amp;qz&amp;qz&amp;0&amp;qz&amp;0&amp;0&amp;z&amp;q^2z&amp;0 .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nDirect substitution gives the same value under either bracketing, proving associativity by trilinearity.</p><p>The positive-degree span \\(N\\) is a nilpotent ideal, \\(N^5=0\\), and \\(C/N\\simeq k\\times k\\). Thus \\(N=\\operatorname{rad}C\\). Finally,\n\\[\n(ut)u=(1+q)v,\n\\qquad\nvt=qz,\n\\]\nwhich gives the stated nonzero fourth radical product.</p><p>The corner description recorded in original Remark 4.2 is\n\\[\neCe=k\\langle x,y\\rangle/(x^2,y^2,xy-qyx),\n\\qquad z=yx.\n\\]\nThe right \\(eCe\\)-module \\(fCe\\) has basis \\(t,j\\), with \\(tx=j\\), \\(ty=q^2j\\), and \\(jx=jy=0\\). Writing \\(R=eCe\\), the map\n\\[\nt\\longmapsto x+q^{-1}y,\n\\qquad\nj\\longmapsto q^{-1}z\n\\]\nidentifies it with \\((x+q^{-1}y)R\\). In the notation of the cited Schulz example, this ideal is the first syzygy of \\((x+y)R\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(T=C\\ltimes DC\\) be as in Proposition 2, using the associative algebra established in Lemma 2.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algebra \\(T\\) is symmetric of dimension \\(20\\), with symmetrizing trace\n\\[\n\\lambda(a,\\phi)=\\phi(1).\n\\]\nThe trace-dual of each basis letter is its starred letter. Its radical \\(\\mathfrak r\\) is the span of all basis letters except \\(e,f\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathfrak r^6=0,\n\\qquad\nT/\\mathfrak r\\simeq k\\times k.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The dual actions satisfy the bimodule identities by associativity of \\(C\\), so (4.4) is associative. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\lambda((a,\\phi)(b,\\psi))=\\psi(a)+\\phi(b),\n\\]\nwhich is symmetric and nondegenerate. In particular,\n\\[\n\\lambda(ab^*)=\\delta_{a,b}\n\\]\nfor lower-case basis letters \\(a,b\\). The dual of \\(iCj\\) lies in \\(jLi\\).</p><p>Extend the grading of Lemma 2.1 by\n\\[\n\\deg(a^*)=5-\\deg(a).\n\\]\nFormula (4.3) makes every product homogeneous. All basis vectors except \\(e,f\\) have positive degree, and no degree exceeds \\(5\\). Their span is therefore a nilpotent ideal with quotient \\(k\\times k\\), proving the assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.4; finite data from Appendix A", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(T,\\mathfrak r,I,s\\) from Proposition 2 and Lemma 2.2.</p><p class=\"li\">A four-letter entry \\(abcv\\) in the following table means that the coefficient shown is the coefficient of \\(v\\) in \\(p(a,b,c)\\). All unlisted coefficients are zero:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|l}\n\\text{coefficient}&amp;\\text{input and output letters}\\\\ \\hline\nq^2&amp;\\texttt{EuNT EyZX FNtU JtXZ NtEU NtXV XzJT ZxEY tEun tXuf}\\\\\nq^2&amp;\\texttt{tXvn tXzj tuFn tuNf tvNn uFNT vNNT vtJu xEyz zJtx}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{EuUE EuVX EvVE ExXE EyYE EzZE FNnF FVvF FVxU FtTF}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{FtYU Ftun Ftxj JFtX JNtZ JUyX JVyZ JjEE JjTT JjXX}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{NFtU NNtV NUyU NVyV NjJN NjZV NnNN NnVV Ntuf Ntvn}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{Ntzj TtEE TtXX UuFF UuUU UvNF UvVU UxXU Uyun Uyxj}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{UzZU VvNN VvVV VxEU VxXV Vyuf Vyvn Vyzj VzZV XxEE}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{XxXX YuFT YuUY YvNT YvVY YxXY YyEE YyJJ YyTT YyXX}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{YyYY YyZZ YzZY ZxXZ ZzEE ZzJJ ZzTT ZzXX jJFF jJUU}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{jJjj jJnn jYyj jZvn jZzj nNFF nNUU ntTn tJjt tTNN}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{tTVV tTtt tYyt tZzt tzJn uFVX uFty uNFT uNUY uNte}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{uUyy uVye ujJu vNUE vNVX vNty vVyy vtTv xEuv xJFT}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{xJUY xJjx xJte xTtx xXuu xXvv xXyy xXzz xYyx xZye}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{xZzx xuFv xuNu xvNv yxJu yzJv zJNT zJVY zYyz zZuu}\\\\\nq&amp;\\texttt{zZvv zZyy zZzz}\\\\\nq^3&amp;\\texttt{EyJT JtEY tEyj tyJf}\\\\\n1&amp;\\texttt{JNjX JVzX NNjU NVzU NjYU Njun Njxj Ntxt TNtX TVyX}\\\\\n1&amp;\\texttt{Vyxt VzYU Vzun Vzxj YuNJ YuVZ jYun juNn jxJn txTn}\\\\\n1&amp;\\texttt{uNNJ uNVZ uNjy uVzy xJNJ xJVZ xTNT xTVY yxTv zYuv}\\\\\n1&amp;\\texttt{zuNv zxJv}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nEach entry has composable radical inputs and output with the same endpoints.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(H\\in k^\\times\\), let \\(h=h_H:T\\to T\\) fix \\(C\\) and multiply \\(DC\\) by \\(H\\). Define\n\\[\nz_H(a)=\n\\begin{cases}\nHqa,&amp;a\\in\\{u,v,t,E,X,Y,Z,U,V\\},\\\\\n0,&amp;\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\non the radical basis, and extend linearly. Let \\(\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}\\) denote that basis.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cochain \\(p\\) has weight \\(-1\\), is a Hochschild cocycle, and evaluates nontrivially on \\(s\\) in \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^3_T(s,s)\\). For every \\(a,b\\in\\mathfrak r\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}}\np(a,b,h(w))w^*\n=\naz_H(b)+z_H(ab)+z_H(a)h^{-1}(b).\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>There are \\(179\\) entries, with distinct three-letter inputs. In every entry the number of capital input letters is one more than the number of capital output letters, proving the weight assertion.</p><p>Here are the finite coefficient identities verifying the endpoint, cocycle, and boundary assertions. Let \\(\\mathcal B\\) be the twenty-letter basis, and write\n\\[\nab=\\sum_v\\mu^v_{ab}v,\n\\qquad\np(a,b,c)=\\sum_vp^v_{abc}v.\n\\]\nThe lower-case coefficients are the multiplication table and idempotent actions. For lower-case \\(a,b,c\\), the remaining coefficients are determined by\n\\[\n\\mu^{c^*}_{a,b^*}=\\mu^b_{ca},\n\\qquad\n\\mu^{c^*}_{b^*,a}=\\mu^b_{ac},\n\\qquad\n\\mu^v_{a^*,b^*}=0.\n\\tag{A.1}\n\\]\nUnspecified coefficients are zero, and every coefficient belongs to \\(\\mathbb F_2[q]\\).</p><p>For every \\(a,b,c,d\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}\\) and \\(v\\in\\mathcal B\\), substitution gives\n\\[\n0=\n\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal B}\n\\left(\n\\mu^v_{ax}p^x_{bcd}\n+\\mu^x_{ab}p^v_{xcd}\n+\\mu^x_{bc}p^v_{axd}\n+\\mu^x_{cd}p^v_{abx}\n+p^x_{abc}\\mu^v_{xd}\n\\right).\n\\tag{A.2}\n\\]\nThis is the coefficient of \\(v\\) in the normalized Hochschild differential. It covers all \\(18^4=104\\,976\\) radical four-words; \\(15\\,250\\) have composable endpoints, and the other equations vanish by the corners.</p><p>For the boundary identity, let \\(\\epsilon_a=0\\) on lower-case letters and \\(\\epsilon_a=1\\) on capital letters, and put\n\\[\n\\gamma_a=\n\\begin{cases}\nq,&amp;a\\in\\{u,v,t,E,X,Y,Z,U,V\\},\\\\\n0,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFor every \\(a,b\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}\\) and \\(v\\in\\mathcal B\\), the table gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{w\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}\\\\\\epsilon_w=0}}\n\\sum_xp^x_{abw}\\mu^v_{x,w^*}\n=\n\\epsilon_b\\gamma_a\\mu^v_{ab},\n\\tag{A.3}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{w\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}\\\\\\epsilon_w=1}}\n\\sum_xp^x_{abw}\\mu^v_{x,w^*}\n=\n\\bigl(\\gamma_b+\\gamma_v+(1-\\epsilon_b)\\gamma_a\\bigr)\\mu^v_{ab}.\n\\tag{A.4}\n\\]\nThere are \\(18^2=324\\) input pairs. These are the constant and linear coefficients in \\(H\\) of (4.6): the left side contributes \\(H^{\\epsilon_w}\\), while the three right-side terms contribute\n\\[\nH\\gamma_b\\mu^v_{ab},\n\\qquad\nH\\gamma_v\\mu^v_{ab},\n\\qquad\nH^{1-\\epsilon_b}\\gamma_a\\mu^v_{ab}.\n\\]\nThus (4.6) holds for an indeterminate \\(H\\), hence for the required parameters. These are polynomial identities in characteristic two and require no specialization.</p><p>For nonzero evaluation, use the two-sided-simple bar complex \\(s^r\\otimes_TP\\otimes_Ts\\). The element\n\\[\n\\zeta=q^2[t|x|J]+[t|y|J]\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nhas endpoint \\(f\\) on each side. Outer radical actions vanish, and\n\\[\ntx=j,\\qquad ty=q^2j,\\qquad xJ=T,\\qquad yJ=q^2T.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nd\\zeta\n=\nq^2([j|J]+[t|T])+q^2[j|J]+q^2[t|T]\n=\n0.\n\\]\nThe table gives\n\\[\np(t,x,J)=0,\n\\qquad\np(t,y,J)=q^3f,\n\\]\nso evaluation on \\(\\zeta\\) is \\(q^3\\neq0\\). Every \\(T\\)-linear map \\(P\\otimes_Ts\\to s\\) factors through the left simple quotient and therefore pairs with this two-sided-simple bar complex. A coboundary pairs to zero with its cycles. Hence the evaluated class is nonzero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)\\), and let \\(C,T=C\\ltimes DC,s\\), and \\(p\\) be the algebra, simple module, and cocycle of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nA=T\\otimes_kT,\n\\qquad\nX=s\\otimes_ks.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p_1,p_2\\) be the external copies of the Hochschild class \\(p\\) on the two factors, and write \\(\\tau_i=p_i(X)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Stable shifts and stable Hom are as in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algebra \\(A\\) is symmetric of dimension \\(400\\). The simple left \\(A\\)-module \\(X\\) is nonprojective, and\n\\[\n\\bigoplus_{a\\geq0}\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,X[a])\n=\nk[\\tau_1,\\tau_2],\n\\qquad\n|\\tau_1|=|\\tau_2|=3.\n\\]\nMultiplication is composition with shifts, and the positive-degree part agrees with the ordinary Ext algebra.</p>", "proof": "<p>The tensor product of the symmetrizing forms on \\(T\\) makes \\(A=T\\otimes T\\) symmetric, and \\(\\dim_kA=400\\).</p><p>Tensor two projective \\(T\\)-resolutions of \\(s\\) over \\(k\\). Each total degree contains only finitely many summands, so this is a resolution of \\(X\\) by finite projective \\(A\\)-modules. The Hom complex is the total tensor product of the two Hom complexes over \\(T\\). Splitting complexes of vector spaces gives the Künneth isomorphism. Tensoring comparison maps identifies multiplication as well; the external maps commute in characteristic two. Lemma 3.2 gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^*_A(X,X)=k[\\tau_1,\\tau_2].\n\\]</p><p>The module \\(X\\) has a nonzero positive self-extension and is not projective. Its ordinary endomorphism ring is \\(k\\), since \\(X\\) is one-dimensional. Its identity cannot factor through a projective, as such a factorization would make \\(X\\) a direct summand of that projective. Thus its stable endomorphism ring is also \\(k\\). Positive stable Hom agrees with ordinary Ext, proving the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be the algebra of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s\\) and \\(s^r\\) be its left and right \\(f\\)-simples.</p><p class=\"li\">For every \\(i\\geq0\\), put\n\\[\n\\ell_i=x+q^iy.\n\\]\nThe parameter \\(q\\) satisfies \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(1+q^m\\neq0\\) for every positive integer \\(m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A projective resolution \\(R\\to s\\) has\n\\[\nR_0=Cf,\n\\qquad\nR_i=Ce\\quad(i\\geq1),\n\\]\nwith \\(d_1\\) right multiplication by \\(u\\) and \\(d_{i+2}\\) right multiplication by \\(\\ell_i\\):\n\\[\n\\cdots\\longrightarrow\nCe\\xrightarrow{\\cdot\\ell_1}\nCe\\xrightarrow{\\cdot\\ell_0}\nCe\\xrightarrow{\\cdot u}\nCf\\longrightarrow s\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nFor all \\(i\\geq0\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i_C(s,s)=\n\\begin{cases}\nk,&amp;i=0,\\\\\n0,&amp;i&gt;0,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i_C(s,C)=\n\\begin{cases}\ns^r,&amp;i=2,\\\\\n0,&amp;i\\neq2.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe second identification is an identification of right \\(C\\)-modules.</p>", "proof": "<p>The left ideals \\(Ce,Cf\\) have bases\n\\[\n(e,x,y,z,t,j),\n\\qquad\n(f,u,v,n),\n\\]\nrespectively. The multiplication table gives\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cccccc}\na&amp;e&amp;x&amp;y&amp;z&amp;t&amp;j\\\\ \\hline\nau&amp;u&amp;v&amp;v&amp;0&amp;(1+q)n&amp;0\\\\\na\\ell_i&amp;\\ell_i&amp;q^{i+1}z&amp;z&amp;0&amp;(1+q^{i+2})j&amp;0 .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nRight multiplication by \\(u\\) has image \\(\\langle u,v,n\\rangle=\\operatorname{rad}(Cf)\\) and kernel \\(\\langle\\ell_0,z,j\\rangle\\). Right multiplication by \\(\\ell_i\\) has image \\(\\langle\\ell_i,z,j\\rangle\\) and kernel \\(\\langle\\ell_{i+1},z,j\\rangle\\). In the latter calculation, a kernel vector has zero \\(e\\)- and \\(t\\)-coefficients, and its \\(x,y\\) coefficients satisfy \\(a_y=q^{i+1}a_x\\). This proves exactness at every index.</p><p>Since\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_C(Ce,s)=es=0,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Hom}_C(Cf,s)=fs=k,\n\\]\nthe asserted self-extension groups follow.</p><p>For \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^*_C(s,C)\\), evaluation at the idempotent identifies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_C(Ce,C)=eC,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Hom}_C(Cf,C)=fC.\n\\]\nPrecomposition with right multiplication becomes left multiplication, so the Hom complex is\n\\[\nfC\\xrightarrow{u\\cdot}\neC\\xrightarrow{\\ell_0\\cdot}\neC\\xrightarrow{\\ell_1\\cdot}\neC\\longrightarrow\\cdots.\n\\]\nLeft multiplication by \\(u\\) sends the basis \\((f,t,j,n)\\) to\n\\[\n(u,y+qx,z,v),\n\\]\nwhich is independent. On \\((e,x,y,z,u,v)\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cccccc}\na&amp;e&amp;x&amp;y&amp;z&amp;u&amp;v\\\\ \\hline\n\\ell_i a&amp;\\ell_i&amp;q^iz&amp;qz&amp;0&amp;(1+q^i)v&amp;0 .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nAt \\(i=0\\),\n\\[\n\\ker(\\ell_0\\cdot)=\\langle qx+y,z,u,v\\rangle=\\operatorname{im}(u\\cdot),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{im}(\\ell_0\\cdot)=\\langle\\ell_0,z\\rangle.\n\\]\nFor every \\(i\\geq1\\),\n\\[\n\\ker(\\ell_i\\cdot)=\\langle\\ell_{i-1},z,v\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{im}(\\ell_i\\cdot)=\\langle\\ell_i,z,v\\rangle.\n\\]\nThus degrees zero and one vanish, degree two is the line represented by \\(v\\), and every higher kernel equals the preceding image. Finally,\n\\[\nvf=v,\\qquad ve=0,\n\\]\nand the only possibly nonzero right radical product is \\(vt=qz\\), a boundary. The surviving right module is \\(s^r\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(T=C\\ltimes DC\\) and \\(s\\) be as in Proposition 3. Let \\(\\tau=p(s)\\) be the evaluation of the cocycle in Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>As a graded algebra,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^*_T(s,s)=k[\\tau],\n\\qquad\n|\\tau|=3.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(L=DC\\), and use the resolution \\(R\\) from Lemma 3.1. For a finite projective left \\(C\\)-module \\(V\\), evaluation gives the natural isomorphism\n\\[\nDC\\otimes_CV\n\\longrightarrow\nD\\operatorname{Hom}_C(V,C),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda\\otimes v\\longmapsto\n\\bigl(\\phi\\longmapsto\\lambda(\\phi(v))\\bigr).\n\\]\nApply it termwise to \\(R\\). Exact vector-space duality and Lemma 3.1 show that \\(L\\otimes_CR\\) has homology only in degree two, where it is \\(D(s^r)=s\\). The ideal \\(L\\) acts by zero on the entire complex because \\(L^2=0\\). Good truncations therefore give the \\(T\\)-linear derived identification\n\\[\nL\\otimes_CR\\simeq s[2].\n\\]</p><p>Regard\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow L\\longrightarrow T\\longrightarrow C\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nas a sequence of \\((T,C)\\)-bimodules. Tensoring termwise with \\(R\\) is exact because each \\(R_i\\) is projective; no right flatness of \\(L\\) or \\(T\\) is required. Put \\(\\mathcal I=T\\otimes_CR\\). The resulting triangle is\n\\[\ns[2]\\longrightarrow\\mathcal I\\longrightarrow s\n\\xrightarrow{\\delta}s[3].\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nThe middle complex has homology \\(s\\) in degrees zero and two only. Its terms are finite-dimensional, so good truncation gives an object of \\(\\mathrm D^b(T\\text{-mod})\\).</p><p>Every term of \\(\\mathcal I\\) is finite projective over \\(T\\). Since it is bounded below homologically, it computes derived maps out of it: a map from it to an acyclic complex is nullhomotopic by successive projective lifts starting in its bottom degree. Induction–restriction adjunction gives\n\\[\nB^i:=\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm D^b(T\\text{-mod})}(\\mathcal I,s[i])\n=\nH^i\\operatorname{Hom}_C(R,s)\n=\n\\begin{cases}\nk,&amp;i=0,\\\\\n0,&amp;i\\neq0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nE^i=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm D^b(T\\text{-mod})}(s,s[i]).\n\\]\nThese are ordinary Ext groups for \\(i\\geq0\\) and vanish for \\(i&lt;0\\). Applying \\(\\operatorname{Hom}(-,s[i])\\) to (5.1) gives\n\\[\nB^{i-1}\\longrightarrow E^{i-3}\n\\xrightarrow{\\delta^*}E^i\\longrightarrow B^i.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nFor \\(i=1\\), \\(E^{-2}=B^1=0\\), so \\(E^1=0\\). For every \\(i\\geq2\\), both displayed \\(B\\)-groups vanish; hence \\(\\delta^*\\) is an isomorphism. This includes \\(E^2=0\\), and at \\(i=3\\) it sends \\(\\operatorname{id}_s\\) to the nonzero class \\(\\delta\\).</p><p>For \\(\\beta:s\\to s[i-3]\\),\n\\[\n\\delta^*(\\beta)=\\beta[3]\\circ\\delta.\n\\]\nThus the connecting map is multiplication by \\(\\delta\\) in the Yoneda algebra. Every \\(\\delta^m\\) is nonzero and spans \\(E^{3m}\\), and all other nonnegative degrees vanish. Hence \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^*_T(s,s)=k[\\delta]\\).</p><p>Proposition 2 shows that \\(p(s)\\) is nonzero in degree three by evaluation on the cycle \\(\\zeta\\). It is therefore a nonzero scalar multiple of \\(\\delta\\), and is itself a polynomial generator. This gives the stated normalization \\(\\tau=p(s)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 6.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)\\), \\(A=T\\otimes T\\), \\(X=s\\otimes s\\), and \\(R=A^e\\), with \\(T,s,p\\) as in Propositions 2 and 3.</p><p class=\"li\">The algebras \\(A\\) and \\(R\\) are symmetric.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nH^a=\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,X[a]).\n\\]\nBy Proposition 3,\n\\[\nH^{\\geq0}=k[\\tau_1,\\tau_2],\n\\qquad\n|\\tau_1|=|\\tau_2|=3.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A bimodule is called side-projective if it is projective separately on its left and right \\(A\\)-sides.</p><p class=\"li\">For a bounded-below complex \\(V\\) of finite projective \\(R\\)-modules which is exact in sufficiently high degrees, define its stable tail by\n\\[\n\\langle V\\rangle\n=\n\\bigl(\\operatorname{coker}(V_{N+1}\\to V_N)\\bigr)[N]\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\) be the bar resolution of \\(T\\), with comparison \\(p:P\\to P[3]\\) from Proposition 2. Define\n\\[\nK=\n\\operatorname{Cone}(p:P[-3]\\to P)\n\\otimes_k\n\\operatorname{Cone}(p:P[-3]\\to P),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal C=\\langle K\\rangle.\n\\]\nUse a finite side-projective representative of \\(\\mathcal C\\), whose existence is established in Lemma 4.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(a\\),\n\\[\nW^a:=\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,(\\mathcal C\\otimes_AX)[a])\n=\n\\begin{cases}\nk,&amp;a=0\\text{ or }3,\\\\\n0,&amp;a\\notin\\{0,3\\}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nMoreover, projection to the top cell induces an isomorphism\n\\[\nW^3\\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ }H^{-1}.\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal C_{\\leq1}\\) be the stable tail of the first two filtration levels. By Lemma 4.2, in total cohomological degree \\(a\\), the Hom groups of the three associated levels are\n\\[\nB^a=H^a,\n\\qquad\nD^a=(H^{a-2})^2,\n\\qquad\nE^a=H^{a-4}.\n\\]\nTheir adjacent connecting maps are\n\\[\nd_a:D^a\\longrightarrow B^{a+1},\n\\qquad\nd_a(u,v)=\\tau_1u+\\tau_2v,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nd'_a:E^a\\longrightarrow D^{a+1},\n\\qquad\nd'_a(z)=(\\tau_2z,\\tau_1z).\n\\]\nThe order in the column records that deleting the first slot from the full cell leaves the second singleton, and conversely.</p><p>For \\(m\\geq0\\), with \\(\\mathcal P_j=0\\) for \\(j&lt;0\\), the degreewise Koszul sequence is\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\\mathcal P_{m-1}\n\\xrightarrow{(\\tau_2,\\tau_1)^t}\n\\mathcal P_m^{\\oplus2}\n\\xrightarrow{(\\tau_1,\\tau_2)}\n\\mathcal P_{m+1}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nThe row is surjective because every positive-degree monomial is divisible by one of the variables. The column is injective, and coprimality shows that every relation \\(\\tau_1u+\\tau_2v=0\\) is \\((u,v)=(\\tau_2w,\\tau_1w)\\).</p><p>For negative groups, dualize (6.8) and interchange its middle components. By Lemma 4.1, this is exactly the sequence through \\(D^a\\). At the seam, its terminal zero is the actual group \\(H^2=0\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\ker d_a=\\operatorname{im}d'_{a-1},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{coker}d_{a-1}=\n\\begin{cases}\nk,&amp;a=0,\\\\\n0,&amp;a\\neq0,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n\\ker d'_a=\n\\begin{cases}\nk=H^{-1},&amp;a=3,\\\\\n0,&amp;a\\neq3.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nFor the column kernel, the maps on \\(D\\mathcal P_m\\), \\(m\\geq1\\), are dual to the surjective positive row, hence injective. The remaining source is \\(D\\mathcal P_0=H^{-1}\\), whose products land in \\(H^2=0\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nK_1^a=\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A\n(X,(\\mathcal C_{\\leq1}\\otimes_AX)[a]).\n\\]\nThe first filtration sequence gives\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{coker}d_{a-1}\n\\longrightarrow K_1^a\n\\xrightarrow{q_a}\\ker d_a\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{6.10}\n\\]\nThe top-cell connecting map \\(r_a:E^a\\to K_1^{a+1}\\) satisfies\n\\[\nq_{a+1}r_a=d'_a.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\ker r_a\\subseteq\\ker d'_a\\), and it can be nonzero only for \\(a=3\\). At that index \\(K_1^4=0\\) by (6.10), since \\(D^4=(H^2)^2=0\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\ker r_3=E^3=H^{-1}.\n\\]</p><p>The image of \\(r_{a-1}\\) surjects onto \\(\\ker d_a\\) by (6.9). For \\(a\\neq0\\), the left term of (6.10) is zero, so \\(r_{a-1}\\) is surjective. For \\(a=0\\), \\(K_1^0=k\\), while the incoming source is \\(E^{-1}=H^{-5}=0\\). Thus \\(\\operatorname{coker}r_{a-1}\\) is \\(k\\) exactly at \\(a=0\\).</p><p>The second filtration sequence gives\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{coker}r_{a-1}\n\\longrightarrow W^a\n\\longrightarrow\\ker r_a\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThis proves the profile. At \\(a=3\\), the left term is zero and the right term is all of \\(E^3=H^{-1}\\). Thus the actual top-cell projection is the isomorphism (6.7). The two exact sequences account for the full filtration, including attaching maps not determined by adjacent components.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "the symmetric stable-duality and multiplication calculation in Section 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(A,X,H^a,\\tau_1,\\tau_2\\) from Proposition 4. For \\(m\\geq0\\), let \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) be the degree-\\(m\\) part of \\(k[\\tau_1,\\tau_2]\\) when each variable has degree one.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For finite modules \\(M,N\\) over a symmetric algebra \\(G\\), there is a natural stable duality\n\\[\nD\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_G(M,N)\n\\simeq\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_G(N,M[-1]).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nDH^a\\simeq H^{-a-1},\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nH^{3m}=\\mathcal P_m,\n\\qquad\nH^{-3m-1}=D\\mathcal P_m\n\\qquad(m\\geq0),\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nwith all other groups zero.</p><p>Within negative degrees, the action of each \\(\\tau_i\\) is dual to polynomial multiplication. Its products crossing from negative to nonnegative degree vanish.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a complete resolution \\(Q\\) of \\(M\\), with \\(M=\\operatorname{coker}(Q_1\\to Q_0)\\). For finite projective \\(Q_i\\), symmetry gives\n\\[\nD\\operatorname{Hom}_G(Q_i,N)\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{Hom}_G(N,Q_i).\n\\]\nIt suffices to check this for \\(Q_i=G\\), using \\(DG\\simeq G\\), and then pass to finite sums and summands.</p><p>Dualizing \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_G(Q,N)\\) therefore gives the chain complex \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_G(N,Q)\\). Its degree-zero homology consists of maps into\n\\[\n\\ker(Q_0\\to Q_{-1})=\\Omega M\n\\]\nmodulo maps through \\(Q_1\\). These are exactly projective factorizations, because maps from a projective to \\(\\Omega M\\) lift through \\(Q_1\\twoheadrightarrow\\Omega M\\). Exact vector-space duality proves (6.2), naturally in \\(N\\). Apply shifts to obtain (6.3), and then use Proposition 3 to obtain (6.4).</p><p>Postcomposition by \\(\\tau_i[a]\\) gives \\(H^a\\to H^{a+3}\\). By naturality of (6.2), its transpose, after shifting the source to \\(X\\), is\n\\[\nH^{-a-4}\\longrightarrow H^{-a-1},\n\\qquad\ng\\longmapsto g[3]\\circ\\tau_i.\n\\]\nFor \\(a\\leq-4\\), this is multiplication by \\(\\tau_i\\) in the polynomial ring. Thus the action within negative degrees is dual to polynomial multiplication. The only nonzero possible source crossing from negative to nonnegative degree is \\(H^{-1}\\), whose target is \\(H^2=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.1. The stable-tail conventions and their properties are from Section 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(R=A^e\\), \\(P\\), \\(K\\), and \\(\\langle-\\rangle\\) from Proposition 4. Put\n\\[\nP^{\\mathrm{tot}}=P\\otimes_kP.\n\\]\nFilter the four cone cells by subsets \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,2\\}\\), with cell\n\\[\nP^{\\mathrm{tot}}[-2|S|].\n\\]\nLet \\(K_{\\leq1}\\) denote the first two filtration levels.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The complex \\(K\\) has finite projective \\(R\\)-terms and is exact in degrees greater than zero. For every \\(N\\geq1\\),\n\\[\nC_N=\\operatorname{coker}(K_{N+1}\\to K_N)\n\\]\nis finite and side-projective. Hence \\(\\mathcal C=\\langle K\\rangle\\) has a finite side-projective representative. The same conclusions hold for the first two filtration levels.</p><p>The stable-tail construction is independent of sufficiently large \\(N\\), respects shifts and homotopic tail maps, and converts the filtration into stable triangles. After evaluation on \\(X\\), its stable cells are\n\\[\nX,\\qquad X[-2]^{\\oplus2},\\qquad X[-4],\n\\]\nwith adjacent connecting maps given by the shifted actions of \\(\\tau_1,\\tau_2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a complex \\(V\\) exact in high degrees, write \\(V_N^c=\\operatorname{coker}(V_{N+1}\\to V_N)\\). Exactness gives\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow V_{N+1}^c\\longrightarrow V_N\\longrightarrow V_N^c\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThus \\(V_{N+1}^c\\simeq V_N^c[-1]\\) stably, proving independence of \\(N\\) in (6.5). Chain maps defined in high degrees induce maps of these cokernels, compatibly with increasing \\(N\\) and shifts. A chain homotopy changes the induced bottom map by a factorization through a projective.</p><p>For a degreewise split sequence\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow B\\longrightarrow V\\longrightarrow D\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\nthe high cokernels form a short exact sequence once \\(H_{N+1}(D)=0\\). A possible extra relation in the \\(B\\)-cokernel comes from lifting a cycle in \\(D_{N+1}\\); that cycle is a boundary, and subtracting a lifted boundary leaves a relation already in \\(B\\). Applying Hom from a complete projective resolution gives the stable long exact sequence. If the differential cross term is \\(r:D_j\\to B_{j-1}\\), the connecting map \\(\\langle D\\rangle\\to\\langle B\\rangle[1]\\) is induced by \\(r\\), by lifting along the splitting and taking the \\(B\\)-component of the differential.</p><p>Finite stable shifts of finite side-projective bimodules have finite side-projective representatives. For a syzygy, take a finite \\(R\\)-projective surjection; its kernel sequence splits on each side because its quotient is side-projective. For a cosyzygy, take a finite \\(R\\)-projective-injective embedding; its cokernel sequence splits on each side because its submodule is side-injective, by symmetry of \\(A\\). Similarly, a stable map \\(M\\to N\\) between side-projective bimodules has a finite side-projective cone: represent it by \\(f\\), embed \\(M\\) into a finite \\(R\\)-projective \\(I'\\), and take the cokernel of \\((f,i):M\\to N\\oplus I'\\). Again the sequence splits on each side.</p><p>Every total degree of \\(P^{\\mathrm{tot}}\\) has finitely many summands, each an external tensor of projective bimodules, hence projective over \\(R\\). The complex resolves \\(A\\). In the \\(S\\)-cell of \\(K\\), deletion of \\(i\\in S\\) is the shifted comparison map \\(p_i\\). The associated filtration levels are\n\\[\nP^{\\mathrm{tot}},\n\\qquad\n(P^{\\mathrm{tot}}[-2])^{\\oplus2},\n\\qquad\nP^{\\mathrm{tot}}[-4].\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]\nTheir homology is supported at \\(0,-2,-4\\), respectively. The filtration long exact sequences show that \\(K\\) is exact in positive degrees.</p><p>For \\(N\\geq1\\), its cokernel filtration is exact by the preceding argument. Its factors, with multiplicities \\(1,2,1\\), are\n\\[\n\\operatorname{coker}\n\\bigl(P^{\\mathrm{tot}}_{N+2r+1}\\to P^{\\mathrm{tot}}_{N+2r}\\bigr),\n\\qquad r=0,1,2.\n\\]\nThe augmented resolution of \\(A\\) splits on either \\(A\\)-side because the regular module is projective. Inductively its syzygies are side-projective. The filtration factors, and then their extensions, are therefore side-projective. Finally \\(C_N[N]\\) has a finite side-projective representative by the shift construction. In particular one may take \\(N=1\\) and one finite cosyzygy of \\(C_1\\).</p><p>Evaluation means tensoring over \\(A\\) on the right. A side-projective bimodule gives an exact functor preserving projectives. An \\(R\\)-projective evaluates to an \\(A\\)-projective, because a free bimodule evaluates to \\(A\\otimes_kV\\). The right-split sequences remain exact on evaluation, so the stable shifts and long exact sequences are preserved. The evaluated filtration therefore has the stated cells, and its adjacent connecting maps are the original bimodule comparisons tensored with \\(X\\), namely the shifted \\(\\tau_1,\\tau_2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(k,A,X,R,P,K,\\mathcal C\\) from Propositions 2–4.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(H\\in k^\\times\\). Let \\(h=h_H\\) fix \\(C\\) and multiply \\(DC\\) by \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T_h\\) have its usual left action and right action \\(x\\cdot a=xh(a)\\), and put\n\\[\nU=T_h,\n\\qquad\nQ=P_h,\n\\qquad\nU_H=A_{h\\otimes h},\n\\qquad\nQ^{\\mathrm{tot}}=Q\\otimes_kQ.\n\\]\nThe complex \\(Q\\) is the right-twisted bar resolution of \\(U\\), with augmentation \\(\\epsilon:Q_0\\to U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A formula on bar generators for a map to an untwisted target is extended by\n\\[\n\\psi(a_0[a_1|\\cdots|a_i]a_{i+1})\n=\na_0\\psi([a_1|\\cdots|a_i])h^{-1}(a_{i+1}).\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">In \\(P_0=T\\otimes_IT\\), put\n\\[\n\\xi_H=\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal B}h(w)\\otimes_Iw^*,\n\\qquad\nb(x)=x\\xi_H,\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal B\\) is the basis of \\(T\\). Define\n\\[\n\\beta_H=b\\otimes_kb:\nU_H\\longrightarrow\n\\ker(P^{\\mathrm{tot}}_0\\to A)=\\Omega A.\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a stable bimodule map\n\\[\nf_H:U_H\\longrightarrow\\mathcal C[3]\n\\]\nwhose composite with the top-cell projection \\(\\mathcal C[3]\\to A[-1]\\) is represented by \\(\\beta_H\\). In particular,\n\\[\n\\beta_H(1)=\n\\left(\\sum_wh(w)\\otimes_Iw^*\\right)\n\\otimes\n\\left(\\sum_wh(w)\\otimes_Iw^*\\right).\n\\]\nUnder the canonical identification \\(U_H\\otimes_AX\\simeq X\\), its projection after evaluation sends\n\\[\n1\\longmapsto H^2(f^*\\otimes f^*)\n\\quad\\text{in}\\quad\n\\Omega X=\\operatorname{rad}(A)(f\\otimes f)\\subset A(f\\otimes f).\n\\]\nThis map is nonzero modulo maps through projectives. Consequently,\n\\[\nf_HX\\neq0\n\\quad\\text{in}\\quad\n\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,\\mathcal CX[3]).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>In \\(K[3]\\), the top cell is \\(P^{\\mathrm{tot}}[-1]\\). Put\n\\[\nV=B\\otimes D_b.\n\\]\nThis raises the underlying total degree by one. Lemma 5.2 gives\n\\[\ndV+Vd=(b\\otimes b)\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{tot}},\n\\]\nwhere the right side is supported in degree zero. Thus\n\\[\ndV_0=\\beta_H\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{tot}}_0,\n\\]\nand \\(V\\) satisfies the comparison equation in positive degrees. Since the complex with terms \\(P^{\\mathrm{tot}}_{i+1}\\), augmented to \\(\\Omega A\\), resolves \\(\\Omega A\\), \\(V\\) is a comparison lifting \\(\\beta_H\\).</p><p>Deleting slot \\(1\\) from the top cell contributes \\(pB\\otimes D_b\\) to cell \\(\\{2\\}\\). Deleting slot \\(2\\) contributes zero, because \\(pD_b=0\\). The remaining contribution is canceled using Lemma 5.3.</p><p>Define the graded map \\(\\Phi:Q^{\\mathrm{tot}}\\to K[3]\\) by its cell components\n\\[\n\\Phi_{\\{1,2\\}}=B\\otimes D_b,\n\\qquad\n\\Phi_{\\{2\\}}=G\\otimes D_b,\n\\qquad\n\\Phi_{\\{1\\}}=\\Phi_\\varnothing=0.\n\\tag{7.8}\n\\]\nThe singleton component lowers underlying degree by one, as its shift requires.</p><p>In the top cell, the defect \\(d\\Phi+\\Phi d\\) is \\(D_b\\otimes D_b\\). In cell \\(\\{2\\}\\), deletion of slot \\(1\\) contributes \\(pB\\otimes D_b\\), canceled by \\((dG+Gd)\\otimes D_b\\). Deleting slot \\(2\\) from either nonzero component gives zero because \\(pD_b=0\\). Thus the only defect is \\(D_b\\otimes D_b\\), supported in total source degree zero.</p><p>The map commutes with differentials in every positive total degree. Passing to high cokernels at a common index at least \\(4\\), as in Proposition 4, gives\n\\[\nf_H:U_H\\longrightarrow\\mathcal C[3].\n\\]\nIts top component remains \\(V\\), so its top projection is \\(\\beta_H\\).</p><p>By Lemma 5.4 this projection evaluates nontrivially. Proposition 4 identifies top projection with an isomorphism on \\(W^3\\). Hence \\(f_HX\\) is nonzero, proving the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Section 7.1, “A twisted trace-dual tensor.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(\\xi_H,b,U\\) from Proposition 5, and let \\(\\pi:P_0\\to T\\) be multiplication.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(b:U\\to P_0\\) is a bimodule map with image in \\(\\ker\\pi\\). For each vertex \\(r\\in\\{e,f\\}\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{w\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}\\\\\\operatorname{left}(w)=r}}\nh(w)w^*\n=\nr^*.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\beta_H\\) in Proposition 5 has image in \\(\\Omega A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The associative symmetric trace gives the ordinary coevaluation identity\n\\[\na\\sum_ww\\otimes w^*\n=\n\\sum_ww\\otimes w^*a.\n\\]\nApplying \\(h\\) to the first factor and projecting to \\(\\otimes_I\\) gives\n\\[\nh(a)\\xi_H=\\xi_Ha.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(b(x)=x\\xi_H\\) is a bimodule map from the right-twisted source.</p><p>For a lower-case basis letter \\(w\\), the product \\(ww^*\\) is the dual of its left vertex idempotent, and \\(w^*w\\) is the dual of its right vertex idempotent. Indeed,\n\\[\n(ww^*)(c)=w^*(cw);\n\\]\nthe positive grading makes this zero for \\(c\\in\\operatorname{rad}C\\), and the idempotent values determine it.</p><p>The row dimensions of \\(C\\) are \\(6,4\\), and its column dimensions are also \\(6,4\\). Separating lower-case and upper-case letters gives\n\\[\n\\pi\\xi_H\n=\n\\sum_{w\\text{ lower-case}}ww^*\n+\nH\\sum_{w\\text{ lower-case}}w^*w\n=\n0\n\\]\nin characteristic two. The same calculation at each left vertex gives (7.3): the full sum is zero, and the omitted idempotent term is \\(rr^*=r^*\\).</p><p>Taking the external square gives \\(\\beta_H=b\\otimes b\\), and its image lies in \\(\\ker(P^{\\mathrm{tot}}_0\\to A)\\) because \\(\\pi b=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(Q,P,b\\) from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_b:Q\\to P\\) be \\(b\\epsilon\\) in degree zero and zero in positive degrees.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(i\\geq0\\), define \\(B_i:Q_i\\to P_{i+1}\\) by\n\\[\nB_i([a_1|\\cdots|a_i])\n=\n\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}}\n[a_1|\\cdots|a_i|h(w)]w^*,\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nretaining only composable terms and extending by (7.1). Put \\(B_{-1}=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On the nonnegative resolutions,\n\\[\ndB+Bd=D_b.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(a\\in\\mathfrak r\\) with right vertex \\(r\\), project the first factor of\n\\[\na\\xi_H=\\xi_Hh^{-1}(a)\n\\]\nto \\(\\mathfrak r\\) along \\(I\\). Removing idempotent terms leaves\n\\[\n\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}}ah(w)\\otimes w^*\n+\n\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}}h(w)\\otimes w^*h^{-1}(a)\n=\na\\otimes r^*,\n\\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nwith the appropriate vertex restrictions. The omitted term on the original left side is \\(a\\otimes r^*\\); on the right, the omitted first factors lie in \\(I\\) and disappear under projection.</p><p>For \\(i&gt;0\\), expand \\(dB_i+B_{i-1}d\\) on a bar generator. Multiplications strictly before the old final bar cancel in pairs. The two terms adjacent to the old final letter sum to \\([a_1|\\cdots|a_i]r^*\\) by (7.6). The last multiplication in \\(dB_i\\) gives the same expression by (7.3), so these cancel.</p><p>At degree zero, on \\(r[]r\\),\n\\[\ndB_0(r[]r)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{w\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}\\\\\\operatorname{left}(w)=r}}\nh(w)[]w^*\n+\nr[]\n\\sum_{\\substack{w\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}\\\\\\operatorname{left}(w)=r}}\nh(w)w^*.\n\\]\nThe second term is \\(r[]r^*\\) by (7.3). It restores the omitted idempotent term of \\(\\xi_H\\), giving \\(r\\xi_H=b\\epsilon(r[]r)\\). Bimodule linearity gives the identity in every degree-zero element.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(p:P\\to P[3]\\) from Proposition 2 and \\(B:Q_i\\to P_{i+1}\\) from Lemma 5.2. Then \\(pB:Q\\to P[2]\\) is a chain map.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are bimodule maps\n\\[\nG_i:Q_i\\longrightarrow P_{i-1}\n\\qquad(i\\geq0)\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\npB=dG+Gd.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(z':Q_1\\to T\\) be the cochain \\(z'([a])=z_H(a)\\), extended by (7.1). Its boundary on \\([a|b]\\) is\n\\[\naz'(b)+z'(ab)+z'(a)h^{-1}(b).\n\\]\nBy (4.6), this equals\n\\[\n\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal B_{\\mathfrak r}}p(a,b,h(w))w^*,\n\\]\nthe augmentation of \\((pB)_2\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\pi(pB)_2=z'd:Q_2\\longrightarrow T.\n\\tag{7.7}\n\\]</p><p>Set \\(G_0=0\\), and choose \\(G_1:Q_1\\to P_0\\) with \\(\\pi G_1=z'\\), using projectivity of \\(Q_1\\). By (7.7), \\((pB)_2+G_1d\\) has image in \\(\\ker\\pi=\\operatorname{im}(P_1\\to P_0)\\). Projectivity of \\(Q_2\\) gives \\(G_2\\) satisfying\n\\[\ndG_2=(pB)_2+G_1d.\n\\]</p><p>Suppose \\(i\\geq3\\) and the equation is established below \\(i\\). Since \\(pB\\) is a chain map,\n\\[\nd\\bigl((pB)_i+G_{i-1}d\\bigr)\n=\n(pB)_{i-1}d+\n\\bigl((pB)_{i-1}+G_{i-2}d\\bigr)d\n=\n0.\n\\]\nExactness of \\(P\\) in positive degree and projectivity of \\(Q_i\\) lift this map through \\(d:P_{i-1}\\to P_{i-2}\\), giving \\(G_i\\). The equations in degrees zero and one are zero because the targets of \\(pB\\) have negative index and \\(G_0=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 7.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(\\beta_H:U_H\\to\\Omega A\\) from Proposition 5 and Lemma 5.1. Identify \\(U_H\\otimes_AX\\simeq X\\) using the character of \\(X\\), which is fixed by \\(h\\otimes h\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After evaluation, \\(\\beta_HX\\) sends\n\\[\n1\\longmapsto H^2(f^*\\otimes f^*)\n\\quad\\text{in}\\quad\n\\Omega X\\subset A(f\\otimes f).\n\\]\nThis map does not factor through a projective \\(A\\)-module.</p>", "proof": "<p>In one slot, tensoring \\(\\xi_H\\) with the simple leaves only the term whose right coefficient is \\(f\\). That term has \\(w=f^*\\), and its value is \\(Hf^*\\). Taking the external square gives the displayed value. The cover sequence stays exact after evaluation because it splits on the right; its evaluated degree-zero term is the projective cover \\(A(f\\otimes f)\\), with kernel \\(\\operatorname{rad}(A)(f\\otimes f)\\).</p><p>Suppose a map\n\\[\nX\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{rad}(A)(f\\otimes f)\n\\]\nfactors through a projective. It then factors through a finite free module. Each component \\(X\\to A\\) has image in the left socle because \\(\\operatorname{rad}(A)X=0\\).</p><p>In a symmetric algebra, the left socle also annihilates the radical on the right. If \\(\\operatorname{rad}(A)u=0\\), then for \\(j\\in\\operatorname{rad}(A)\\) and \\(a\\in A\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(auj)=\\operatorname{tr}(jau)=0,\n\\]\nso nondegeneracy gives \\(uj=0\\). A map \\(A\\to\\operatorname{rad}(A)(f\\otimes f)\\) is right multiplication by its value at \\(1\\), an element of \\(\\operatorname{rad}(A)\\), and therefore kills the left socle. Every such factorization is zero.</p><p>The displayed map has nonzero value \\(H^2(f^*\\otimes f^*)\\), so it cannot factor through a projective.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 8.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k,A,X,R=A^e,K,\\mathcal C\\) be as above, and put\n\\[\nC_1=\\operatorname{coker}(K_2\\to K_1),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal C=C_1[1]\n\\]\nin the stable category.</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite left \\(R\\)-module \\(M\\), use\n\\[\nj_M:M\\longrightarrow I_R(M)=\\operatorname{Hom}_k(R,M),\n\\qquad\nj_M(m)(t)=tm,\n\\qquad\n(r\\phi)(t)=\\phi(tr),\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\nand define \\(\\Sigma_RM=\\operatorname{coker}j_M\\). Put\n\\[\n\\mathcal Y=\\Sigma_R^4C_1.\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(i=1,2\\), let\n\\[\n\\sigma_i=h_{H_i}\\otimes h_{H_i},\n\\qquad\nU_i=A_{\\sigma_i}.\n\\]\nUse \\(U_i\\otimes_AX\\simeq X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose actual representatives \\(g_i:U_i\\to\\mathcal Y\\) of the stable maps \\(H_i^{-2}f_{H_i}\\) from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q=R\\otimes_k\\mathcal Y\\), and let \\(\\pi:Q\\to\\mathcal Y\\) be \\(\\pi(r\\otimes y)=ry\\). Define \\(F\\) by\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow F\\longrightarrow U_1\\oplus U_2\\oplus Q\n\\xrightarrow{(g_1,g_2,\\pi)}\n\\mathcal Y\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]\nLet \\(\\rho_i:F\\to U_i\\) be the two projections.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nH^a=\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,X[a]),\n\\qquad\nV^a=\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,FX[a]).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The bimodule \\(F\\) is finite and projective on both \\(A\\)-sides. There is a unique \\(v\\in V^0\\) whose two projections are \\(\\operatorname{id}_X\\). For this \\(v\\), define\n\\[\n\\delta^a:H^a\\oplus H^a\\longrightarrow V^a,\n\\qquad\n(g,j)\\longmapsto F(g)v+v[a]j.\n\\tag{8.6}\n\\]\nThen \\(\\delta^0\\) is surjective with one-dimensional kernel, and \\(\\delta^a\\) is an isomorphism for every \\(a&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Finiteness and side-projectivity follow from Lemma 6.1, and \\(v\\) is supplied by Lemma 6.2.</p><p>Naturality of the actual bimodule projections \\(\\rho_i\\), and \\(\\rho_iv=\\operatorname{id}_X\\), give\n\\[\n\\rho_i[a]F(g)v=U_i(g),\n\\qquad\n\\rho_i[a]v[a]j=j.\n\\]\nAt \\(a=0\\), \\(k\\)-linearity gives\n\\[\n\\delta^0(c\\operatorname{id}_X,d\\operatorname{id}_X)=(c+d)v.\n\\]\nIt is surjective with the diagonal line as kernel.</p><p>Let \\(a=3m&gt;0\\). Under (8.5) and Lemma 6.3,\n\\[\n\\delta^{3m}(g,j)\n=\n(H_1^{-m}g+j,\\ H_2^{-m}g+j).\n\\tag{8.7}\n\\]\nThis is the scalar matrix\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nH_1^{-m}&amp;1\\\\\nH_2^{-m}&amp;1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\ntensored with the identity of \\(H^{3m}\\), which has dimension \\(m+1\\). Its determinant is\n\\[\nH_1^{-m}+H_2^{-m}\\neq0.\n\\]\nOtherwise \\(H_1^m=H_2^m\\), contrary to algebraic independence: the distinct monomials remain distinct in the fraction field of \\(\\mathbb F_2[q,H_1,H_2]\\). This holds for every positive \\(m\\), including those divisible by the characteristic. Hence \\(\\delta^{3m}\\) is invertible.</p><p>If \\(a&gt;0\\) is not divisible by \\(3\\), Proposition 3 gives \\(H^a=0\\), and Lemma 6.2 gives \\(V^a=0\\). Thus \\(\\delta^a\\) is an isomorphism in those degrees as well.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Section 8.1, “A finite representative and an exact sequence.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the finite constructions \\(C_1,\\Sigma_R,\\mathcal Y,U_i,g_i,Q,F,\\rho_i\\) in Proposition 6. Let\n\\[\nW^a=\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,\\mathcal CX[a])\n\\]\nas in Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The module \\(\\mathcal Y\\) is a finite side-projective representative of \\(\\mathcal C[3]\\). The representatives \\(g_i\\) can be chosen as stated, and their evaluations equal the same nonzero \\(w\\in W^3\\).</p><p>The sequence (8.3) splits on each \\(A\\)-side, so \\(F\\) is finite and side-projective. On evaluation at \\(X\\), it gives the stable triangle\n\\[\nFX\\longrightarrow X\\oplus X\n\\xrightarrow{(w,w)}\n\\mathcal CX[3]\n\\longrightarrow FX[1].\n\\tag{8.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Evaluation at \\(1\\) proves that \\(j_M\\) is injective. Since \\(R\\) is symmetric,\n\\[\nI_R(M)\\simeq DR\\otimes_kM\\simeq R\\otimes_kM\n\\]\nas left \\(R\\)-modules, so it is finite projective. If \\(M\\) is side-projective, its sequence defining \\(\\Sigma_RM\\) splits on each side because \\(A\\) is self-injective and \\(M\\) is side-injective. Thus \\(\\Sigma_RM\\) remains side-projective and represents \\(M[1]\\). Proposition 4 therefore makes \\(\\mathcal Y=\\Sigma_R^4C_1\\) a finite side-projective representative of \\(\\mathcal C[3]\\).</p><p>Concretely, the tail map in Proposition 5 at index \\(4\\) induces\n\\[\n\\Omega_R^4U_H\\longrightarrow C_1,\n\\]\nbecause\n\\[\n\\operatorname{coker}(K[3]_5\\to K[3]_4)\n=\n\\operatorname{coker}(K_2\\to K_1).\n\\]\nTaking four cosyzygies gives \\(U_H\\to\\mathcal Y\\), using\n\\[\n\\Sigma_R^4\\Omega_R^4U_H\\simeq U_H\n\\]\nstably. It therefore has an actual bimodule-map representative.</p><p>Each \\(U_i\\) is free of rank one on both sides. Proposition 5 gives top projections \\(H_i^2\\beta_0\\) after evaluation, for one fixed nonzero\n\\[\n\\beta_0\\in\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,X[-1]).\n\\]\nBy Proposition 4, top projection\n\\[\nW^3\\longrightarrow\\underline{\\operatorname{Hom}}_A(X,X[-1])\n\\]\nis an isomorphism. Rescaling by \\(H_i^{-2}\\) makes the evaluated maps the same nonzero \\(w\\in W^3\\).</p><p>The map \\(\\pi:Q\\to\\mathcal Y\\) is surjective, so (8.3) defines a finite module \\(F\\). Since \\(\\mathcal Y\\) is projective on each side, the sequence splits on each side. Hence \\(F\\) is side-projective.</p><p>Tensoring (8.3) with \\(X\\) preserves exactness. The module \\(Q\\otimes_AX\\) is projective over \\(A\\), since evaluation of a free \\(A^e\\)-module is free over \\(A\\), and evaluation preserves direct summands. Removing this projective summand gives the stable triangle (8.4).</p><p>Tensoring with \\(F\\) is exact because \\(F\\) is right projective, and preserves projectives because \\(F\\) is left projective. The maps \\(\\rho_i\\) give natural transformations from this stable functor to tensoring with \\(U_i\\), compatibly with shifts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(F,X,H^a,V^a,\\rho_i\\) from Proposition 6 and the stable triangle (8.4) from Lemma 6.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a unique \\(v\\in V^0\\) whose two projections are \\(\\operatorname{id}_X\\), and it spans \\(V^0\\). For every \\(a&gt;0\\), projection gives an isomorphism\n\\[\n(\\rho_1[a],\\rho_2[a]):\nV^a\\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ }(H^a)^2.\n\\tag{8.5}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Applying stable Hom from \\(X\\) to (8.4) gives\n\\[\n(H^{a-1})^2\n\\longrightarrow W^{a+2}\n\\longrightarrow V^a\n\\longrightarrow(H^a)^2\n\\longrightarrow W^{a+3}.\n\\]\nFor \\(a=0\\), \\(W^2=0\\), and the last map is\n\\[\nk^2\\longrightarrow W^3,\n\\qquad\n(c,d)\\longmapsto(c+d)w.\n\\]\nIt is surjective. Thus \\(V^0\\) identifies with the diagonal line in \\(k^2\\), giving the unique \\(v\\) and the spanning assertion.</p><p>For \\(a=1\\), the preceding map \\((H^0)^2\\to W^3\\) is this same surjection. It kills the possible connecting term \\(W^3\\to V^1\\), and \\(W^4=0\\). Therefore (8.5) is an isomorphism in degree one.</p><p>For \\(a\\geq2\\), both \\(W^{a+2}\\) and \\(W^{a+3}\\) vanish by Proposition 4, so the isomorphism follows directly.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For \\(H\\in\\{H_1,H_2\\}\\), put\n\\[\nU_H=A_{h_H\\otimes h_H}.\n\\]\nUse the canonical identification \\(U_H\\otimes_AX\\simeq X\\), and let \\(H^{3m}\\) denote the stable self-extension space of \\(X\\) from Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Tensoring with \\(U_H\\) acts on the entire space \\(H^{3m}\\) as multiplication by \\(H^{-m}\\) for every \\(m\\geq0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The inverse automorphism comes from the right-twist convention. For an automorphism \\(h\\) of \\(T\\) and a left module \\(M\\), the balanced map\n\\[\nT_h\\otimes_TM\\longrightarrow{}_{h^{-1}}M,\n\\qquad\na\\otimes m\\longmapsto h^{-1}(a)m\n\\]\nis an isomorphism, where the target action is\n\\[\nb\\cdot m=h^{-1}(b)m.\n\\]\nFor \\(M=s\\), this is the canonical simple identification.</p><p>In the normalized bar resolution of \\(s\\), a comparison into its \\(T_h\\)-tensor image applies \\(h^{-1}\\) to every bar letter. It commutes with the differential because \\(h^{-1}\\) preserves products; the first multiplication agrees by balancing, and the last action agrees because \\(h\\) fixes the character of \\(s\\).</p><p>The simple-valued degree-three cocycle obtained from \\(p\\) has exactly one dual-ideal input in every nonzero component. Indeed, \\(p\\) lowers weight by one, and only weight-zero outputs act nontrivially on \\(s\\). The comparison therefore multiplies the cocycle, and its class \\(\\tau\\), by \\(H^{-1}\\).</p><p>The tensor functor preserves Yoneda products. Both factors in \\(A\\) use the same \\(H\\), so \\(\\tau_1,\\tau_2\\) each acquire \\(H^{-1}\\). Every basis monomial\n\\[\n\\tau_1^r\\tau_2^{m-r}\n\\]\nof \\(H^{3m}\\) acquires \\(H^{-m}\\). Linearity gives the assertion on the whole homogeneous piece.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 9.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)\\), let \\(A=T\\otimes T\\) be the algebra of Proposition 3, and let \\(F\\) be the finite bimodule of Proposition 6. Put\n\\[\n\\Lambda=\\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;0\\\\F&amp;A\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algebra \\(\\Lambda\\) is noncommutative, and\n\\[\n\\Lambda/\\operatorname{rad}\\Lambda\\simeq k^8,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rad}(\\Lambda)^4\\neq0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(J_T=\\operatorname{rad}T\\). Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\nJ_T=\\operatorname{rad}C\\oplus DC,\n\\qquad\nT/J_T\\simeq k^2.\n\\]\nThe ideal\n\\[\nJ_T\\otimes T+T\\otimes J_T\n\\]\nin \\(A\\) is nilpotent: its two summand ideals commute and are nilpotent. Its quotient is \\(k^4\\), so it is \\(J_A=\\operatorname{rad}A\\).</p><p>In \\(\\Lambda\\), the ideal\n\\[\nJ_\\Lambda=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nJ_A&amp;0\\\\\nF&amp;J_A\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nis nilpotent. A product has at most one off-diagonal factor, with powers of \\(J_A\\) on its two sides, so sufficiently long products vanish. Its quotient is \\(k^4\\oplus k^4\\). A nilpotent ideal with semisimple quotient is the radical, proving the split quotient and the count of eight simple modules.</p><p>Let \\(\\chi:T\\to k\\) be the character of \\(s\\). There are surjective algebra maps\n\\[\n\\Lambda\\longrightarrow A\n\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{id}_T\\otimes\\chi}T\n\\longrightarrow C.\n\\]\nFor a surjection of finite-dimensional algebras, the image of the radical is the radical of the quotient: the image is nilpotent, and quotienting by it gives a quotient of a semisimple algebra.</p><p>In \\(C\\),\n\\[\n(ut)^2=(y+qx)^2=q(1+q)z\\neq0.\n\\]\nSince \\(u,t\\in\\operatorname{rad}C\\), its fourth radical power is nonzero, and so is that of \\(\\Lambda\\). Finally \\(eu=u\\) and \\(ue=0\\) in \\(C\\); thus \\(C\\), and hence its covering algebra \\(\\Lambda\\), is noncommutative.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 9.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(A,X,F,v\\) from Propositions 3 and 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Form \\(\\Lambda,Z\\) by applying Proposition 1 to these data.</p><p class=\"li\">For a field extension \\(K/k\\), put\n\\[\n\\Lambda_K=K\\otimes_k\\Lambda,\n\\qquad\nZ_K=K\\otimes_kZ.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every field extension \\(K/k\\), the module \\(Z_K\\) is finite, nonprojective, and Gorenstein-projective, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i_{\\Lambda_K}(Z_K,Z_K)\n=\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i_{\\Lambda_K}(Z_K,\\Lambda_K)\n=\n0\n\\qquad(i&gt;0).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\Lambda_K/\\operatorname{rad}\\Lambda_K\\simeq K^8,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rad}(\\Lambda_K)^4\\neq0.\n\\]\nThus all conclusions of the flagship theorem persist under scalar extension.</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 1 and 6 give the asserted module properties over \\(k\\), and Proposition 7 gives the radical assertions.</p><p>For finite \\(\\Lambda\\)-modules \\(M,N\\), take a resolution of \\(M\\) with finite projective terms. Finite projectivity gives, termwise,\n\\[\nK\\otimes_k\\operatorname{Hom}_\\Lambda(P_i,N)\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\Lambda_K}(K\\otimes_kP_i,K\\otimes_kN).\n\\]\nFlatness of \\(K\\) commutes with kernels and images, so\n\\[\nK\\otimes_k\\operatorname{Ext}^i_\\Lambda(M,N)\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{Ext}^i_{\\Lambda_K}(M_K,N_K)\n\\qquad(i\\geq0).\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\nThis proves both Ext vanishings.</p><p>The totally acyclic complex defining \\(Z\\) remains exact after tensoring with \\(K\\). The same termwise Hom isomorphism, with target \\(\\Lambda\\), shows that its dual over \\(\\Lambda_K\\) remains exact. Thus \\(Z_K\\) is Gorenstein-projective.</p><p>Choose a finite projective surjection \\(P\\twoheadrightarrow Z\\), with kernel \\(N\\). It does not split because \\(Z\\) is nonprojective. Its nonzero class in \\(\\operatorname{Ext}^1_\\Lambda(Z,N)\\) stays nonzero under (9.1), since field extension is faithful. The extended sequence therefore does not split, so \\(Z_K\\) is nonprojective.</p><p>The radical ideal in Proposition 7 stays nilpotent and has quotient \\(K^8\\), so it is the radical of \\(\\Lambda_K\\). Its nonzero fourth power stays nonzero. This proves the remaining assertions.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 7, "cite"], [1, 23, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 0, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 8, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [3, 10, "cite"], [3, 13, "cite"], [3, 16, "cite"], [3, 22, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [7, 0, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [7, 18, "cite"], [7, 21, "cite"], [7, 22, "cite"], [7, 23, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 0, "cite"], [10, 13, "cite"], [10, 19, "cite"], [10, 20, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 18, "cite"], [13, 19, "cite"], [14, 17, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 13, "cite"], [17, 13, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"], [18, 22, "cite"], [18, 23, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 18, "cite"], [21, 18, "cite"], [22, 0, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 0, "cite"]]});
